q("ws-201", C, 4, RD, "Ice is heated in a beaker and its temperature is recorded as shown. In which parts does the water gain heat but its temperature stay the same?", "B and D", [W("A and C", "wrong-reading", "The temperature rises in these parts."), W("C and E", "wrong-reading", "The temperature rises in these parts."), W("A, C and E", "wrong-reading", "The temperature rises in all of these parts.")], ["The flat parts, B and D, are where the heat changes the state."], CV)
q("ws-202", C, 4, RD, "Ice is heated in a beaker and its temperature is recorded as shown. In which state is the water at 21 minutes?", "Water vapour only", [W("Liquid water only", "wrong-reading", "All the water has boiled away by then."), W("Liquid water and water vapour", "wrong-reading", "That is the boiling stage, which ended at 20 minutes."), W("Ice and water vapour", "wrong-reading", "The ice melted long before this.")], ["After the flat boiling part is over, the temperature rises again and only water vapour remains."], CV)
q("ws-203", C, 4, RD, "Ice is heated in a beaker and its temperature is recorded as shown. What can be seen in the beaker at 15 minutes?", "Bubbles rising through water that is still there", [W("Only ice cubes still floating in cold water", "wrong-reading", "The ice melted by 6 minutes."), W("Only clear gas, with no liquid water left", "wrong-reading", "The water has not all boiled away yet."), W("Water with no bubbles, which is still getting hotter", "wrong-reading", "Water at 100 " + D + " is boiling, so it has bubbles.")], ["At 15 minutes the water is boiling at 100 " + D + ", so there are bubbles and liquid is left."], CV)
q("ws-204", C, 4, CS, "Ice cubes at 0 " + D + " are put into hot tea at 80 " + D + " and the tea is stirred until all the ice has melted. Which describes the final temperature of the tea?", "Between 0 " + D + " and 80 " + D, [W("Exactly 0 " + D + ", the same as the ice", "wrong-idea", "There was much more heat in the hot tea than the ice needed."), W("Exactly 80 " + D + ", as it was before", "wrong-idea", "The tea loses some heat to the ice, so it cools."), W("Above 80 " + D + ", as heat was added", "reversed", "The tea cannot become hotter by adding ice.")], ["The tea loses heat to the ice, which melts and warms up, so the final temperature is between 0 " + D + " and 80 " + D + "."], None)
q("ws-205", C, 4, FT, "Mei Ling wants to find out how the mass of ice affects the time it takes to melt in a warm room. Which is the measured variable?", "The time taken for the ice to melt", [W("The mass of ice put in each beaker", "wrong-variable", "This is the variable she changes on purpose."), W("The temperature of the warm room", "wrong-variable", "This should be kept the same."), W("The type of container that is used", "wrong-variable", "This should be kept the same.")], ["She changes the mass and measures the time taken."], None)
q("ws-206", C, 3, CS, "A beaker of crushed ice and water is left on a warm table. The thermometer in the beaker is read every minute while some ice is still left. What does it show?", "A reading of 0 " + D + " all the time", [W("A reading that slowly rises above 0 " + D, "wrong-idea", "Heat from the room melts the ice instead of warming it."), W("A reading that falls below 0 " + D, "wrong-idea", "The ice is gaining heat, not losing it."), W("A reading that rises to the room temperature at once", "wrong-idea", "While ice remains, the mixture stays at 0 " + D + ".")], ["The heat gained is used to melt ice, so the temperature stays at 0 " + D + " until the ice is gone."], None)
q("ws-207", C, 2, CS, "More droplets form on the outside of a cold can on a very humid day than on a dry day. Why?", "The air has more water vapour that can condense", [W("The air is hotter and gives more heat to the can", "wrong-idea", "Heat gained by the can does not make droplets."), W("The can makes more water from the cold", "wrong-idea", "A can cannot make water."), W("More ice melts inside the can", "wrong-idea", "The droplets come from outside the can.")], ["More water vapour in the air means more of it condenses on the cold can."], None)
q("ws-208", C, 2, CS, "A girl walks from an air-conditioned room into the warm, humid air outside. Her spectacles become covered with a fine mist. Why?", "Water vapour in the warm air condenses on the cold lenses", [W("Water vapour in her breath freezes on the lenses", "wrong-idea", "The lenses are cold, but not below 0 " + D + "."), W("The lenses make water when they warm up", "wrong-idea", "Glass cannot make water."), W("Water inside the lenses boils away to the surface", "wrong-idea", "Glass does not contain water.")], ["The lenses are cooler than the air, so water vapour touching them loses heat and condenses."], None)
q("ws-209", C, 2, CS, "Two cans in a 30 " + D + " room each hold water. Can A has iced water. Can B has water at 30 " + D + ". Droplets form only on Can A. Why?", "The surface of A is cold enough to make water vapour lose heat", [W("Can B has no water vapour in the air around it", "wrong-idea", "Both cans are surrounded by the same air."), W("Can A gives out more heat to the room than Can B does", "reversed", "Can A is colder, so it gains heat."), W("The water in Can B has already evaporated and boiled away", "wrong-idea", "Water at 30 " + D + " does not boil.")], ["Only the cold surface cools the nearby air enough for water vapour to condense."], None)
q("ws-210", C, 2, CS, "A sealed bottle of iced water is weighed before and after droplets form on its outside. The total mass has gone up. What does this show?", "The water in the droplets came from the air", [W("The water in the droplets came from inside the bottle", "wrong-idea", "The mass would not go up if water only moved from inside the bottle."), W("The ice in the bottle melted into the droplets", "wrong-idea", "The bottle is sealed, so melting ice stays inside."), W("The droplets weigh nothing", "wrong-idea", "Water drops have mass.")], ["Extra mass can only come from outside the sealed bottle, so the droplets came from water vapour in the air."], None)
q("ws-211", C, 4, FT, "Aisha wants to find out whether the starting temperature of the water affects how fast an ice cube melts. Which set-up is a fair test?", "Two beakers of 100 mL of water at 20 " + D + " and 40 " + D + ", each with an identical ice cube", [W("Two beakers of water at 20 " + D + " and 40 " + D + ", with one ice cube and one crushed ice", "unfair", "The ice differs, so more than one thing changes."), W("A beaker of 100 mL of water at 20 " + D + " and a beaker of 200 mL of water at 40 " + D, "unfair", "The amount of water also changes."), W("One beaker of water at 20 " + D + " tested for a long time and another tested for a short time", "unfair", "The time is not the thing to be changed.")], ["Only the starting temperature of the water is changed."], None)
q("ws-212", C, 1, CS, "Which of the following is true for ice, liquid water and water vapour?", "They all have mass and take up space", [W("They all keep their own shape in any container", "wrong-idea", "Only ice keeps its own shape."), W("They all can flow from one container to another", "wrong-idea", "Ice does not flow."), W("They all have a fixed volume that never changes", "wrong-idea", "Water vapour spreads out to fill its container.")], ["All three are matter, so they have mass and take up space."], None)
q("ws-213", C, 3, RD, "A tube of each substance is placed in a water bath at 100 " + D + ". Which substance that is a liquid at 20 " + D + " will boil?", "P", [W("Q", "wrong-reading", "Q boils at 357 " + D + ", far above 100 " + D + "."), W("R", "wrong-reading", "R is a solid at 20 " + D + " and boils at 2750 " + D + "."), W("S", "wrong-reading", "S is already a gas at 20 " + D + ", so it is not a liquid.")], ["Only P is a liquid at 20 " + D + " with a boiling point (78 " + D + ") below 100 " + D + "."], T2)
q("ws-214", C, 3, RD, "The table shows the melting points and boiling points of four substances. Which substance is a liquid at -100 " + D + "?", "P", [W("Q", "wrong-reading", "-100 " + D + " is below the melting point of Q, so Q is solid."), W("R", "wrong-reading", "-100 " + D + " is below the melting point of R, so R is solid."), W("S", "wrong-reading", "-100 " + D + " is above the boiling point of S, so S is a gas.")], ["-100 " + D + " is between -115 " + D + " and 78 " + D + ", so P is a liquid."], T2)
q("ws-215", C, 2, CS, "A student holds a cold metal plate above the spout at X in the kettle. Drops of water form on the plate. What does this show about X?", "Water vapour is present there", [W("Tiny drops of water are already there", "wrong-idea", "If drops were there, X would look misty."), W("Ice is present there", "wrong-idea", "The air at the spout is too hot for ice."), W("Only air is present there", "wrong-idea", "The drops need a supply of water.")], ["Invisible water vapour cooled and condensed on the cold plate."], KE)
q("ws-216", C, 2, CS, "Few droplets form under the lid when it is warmed to 100 " + D + " before it is placed over the pot of boiling water. Why?", "The warm lid does not take enough heat from the water vapour", [W("The warm lid gives heat to the water vapour, which then freezes", "wrong-idea", "Water vapour does not freeze at this temperature."), W("The water in the pot has stopped boiling", "wrong-idea", "The pot is still boiling."), W("The warm lid makes the water vapour disappear", "wrong-idea", "Water vapour is still there but is not condensing.")], ["Condensation needs the water vapour to lose heat to a cooler surface."], PL)
q("ws-217", C, 3, RD, "A student heats solid wax and records its temperature as shown. What is the melting point of the wax?", "60 " + D, [W("30 " + D, "wrong-reading", "30 " + D + " is the starting temperature."), W("90 " + D, "wrong-reading", "90 " + D + " is only the final reading."), W("8 " + D, "wrong-reading", "8 is a time in minutes, not a temperature.")], ["The temperature stays at 60 " + D + " while the wax melts."], WX)
q("ws-218", C, 4, RD, "Solid wax is heated and its temperature is recorded as shown. What is the state of the wax at 5 minutes?", "Partly solid and partly liquid", [W("Solid wax only, not yet changed", "wrong-reading", "The wax started to melt at 3 minutes."), W("Liquid wax only, all melted by now", "wrong-reading", "The wax is still melting."), W("Liquid and gas, since it is very hot", "wrong-reading", "The wax has not started to boil.")], ["The flat part from 3 to 8 minutes is the wax melting, so solid and liquid are both present."], WX)
q("ws-219", C, 4, RD, "Water is cooled in a freezer and its temperature is recorded as shown. In which part is the water changing into ice?", "Q", [W("P", "wrong-reading", "In P the water is only cooling down."), W("R", "wrong-reading", "In R all the water has already turned into ice."), W("P and R", "wrong-reading", "The temperature falls in P and R, so no change of state happens.")], ["The flat part at 0 " + D + " is the water freezing, while it loses heat."], CC)
q("ws-220", C, 4, RD, "Water is cooled in a freezer and its temperature is recorded as shown. What is the state of the water in part R?", "Ice only", [W("Liquid water only", "wrong-reading", "That is part P."), W("Ice and liquid water", "wrong-reading", "That is part Q."), W("Liquid water and water vapour", "wrong-reading", "No water vapour is shown in this experiment.")], ["After the water has frozen, the ice only gets colder."], CC)
q("ws-221", C, 4, RD, "The experiment is repeated using half the amount of water in the same freezer. How does the flat part of the graph change?", "It is still at 0 " + D + " but is shorter", [W("It is at a lower temperature and the same length", "wrong-idea", "Water always freezes at 0 " + D + "."), W("It is still at 0 " + D + " and is longer", "reversed", "Less water has less heat to lose, so it freezes sooner."), W("It disappears since the water cools too fast", "wrong-idea", "Every amount of water still freezes at 0 " + D + ".")], ["Water freezes at 0 " + D + ". Less water loses its heat sooner, so the flat part is shorter."], CC)
q("ws-222", C, 2, CS, "On a hot day, ice cubes melt on a plate and the water later dries up. In the diagram, which letters show these changes in order?", "S, then Q", [W("Q, then S", "reversed", "Q is boiling or evaporation, which comes after melting."), W("R, then P", "reversed", "R is freezing and P is condensation."), W("P, then R", "mixed-up", "P is condensation and R is freezing.")], ["Ice melts first, which is S. The water then evaporates, which is Q."], ST("C"))
q("ws-223", C, 2, CS, "Which statement about the change shown by the letter Q is correct?", "Water vapour loses heat and changes into liquid water", [W("Water vapour gains heat and changes into liquid water", "reversed", "Condensation needs heat to be lost."), W("Liquid water loses heat and changes into water vapour", "reversed", "Liquid water becomes a gas by gaining heat."), W("Liquid water gains heat and changes into ice", "mixed-up", "Q is a change between water vapour and liquid water.")], ["Q is condensation in this diagram, in which water vapour loses heat."], ST("B"))
q("ws-224", C, 4, CS, "A tray of ice cubes taken from a freezer at -10 " + D + " is left in a warm room with a thermometer in one cube. Which describes the reading?", "It rises to 0 " + D + " and stays there until the ice has melted", [W("It stays at -10 " + D + " until all of the ice has melted", "wrong-idea", "The ice gains heat, so its temperature rises to 0 " + D + " first."), W("It rises steadily to room temperature", "wrong-idea", "It stops rising at 0 " + D + " while ice is melting."), W("It falls below -10 " + D + " as the ice takes in heat", "reversed", "Gaining heat cannot lower the temperature.")], ["The ice warms to 0 " + D + ", then the heat gained melts it at constant temperature."], None)
q("ws-225", C, 1, CS, "Which statement about water vapour is correct?", "It is in the air around us even though we cannot see it", [W("It is the white mist seen above a boiling kettle", "wrong-idea", "That mist is tiny drops of liquid water."), W("It is a liquid that spreads out in the air", "wrong-idea", "Water vapour is a gas."), W("It has a fixed volume like liquid water", "wrong-idea", "A gas spreads out to fill the space it is in.")], ["Water vapour is an invisible gas that is always in the air."], None)

